---
/*
  The page Ralfs sends in the middle of a conversation. It has to stand on its
  own. Structure and copy: 04-konzept.md section 4.3, page "/how-it-works",
  built on the three pillars in section 2.2.
*/
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import CtaBlock from '../components/CtaBlock.astro';
import Placeholder from '../components/Placeholder.astro';
---

<Base
  title="How it works | Wunder"
  description="Three mechanisms you can check: your own portal, authorised interfaces instead of passwords, and an audit on everyone who writes in your name."
>
  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h1>How the work is actually done.</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        Three mechanisms. Each one is something you can check, not something you have to believe.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" id="portal">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="eyebrow">Mechanism one</p>
      <h2>You see the same screen we do.</h2>
      <p class="lead">
        You see what is happening on your account at any time, without asking us, and you see the
        same number we see.
      </p>
      <p>
        Every post runs through our own portal and we read every number at the source instead of
        estimating it. We built the whole chain ourselves, so there is nothing we would have to tell
        you that we could not show you.
      </p>
      <ol>
        <li>
          Before we start we read what your account did on its own over the last thirty days. That
          number is your starting point.
        </li>
        <li>
          Our posting engine recalculates the thirty day median of your account every night and
          scores every single post against your own median, not against an agency average. A post
          counts as scoreable after seventy two hours of maturity.
        </li>
        <li>
          You log into your own portal and see views, reach and followers over seven, thirty and
          ninety days, the daily curve, the comparison with the period before, the content calendar
          and the strategy your manager wrote.
        </li>
        <li>
          What you do not see there, you do not see on purpose: the identity of individual fans and
          internal team metrics. That line exists so your access is not a side door into other
          people's data.
        </li>
      </ol>
      <div class="portal-hint">
        <Placeholder label="Reduced hint of the portal view, no readable numbers" ratio="wide" />
        <p class="caption">
          A reduced hint, on purpose. The portal itself is not a public demo and stays out of the
          index.
        </p>
      </div>
      <CtaBlock />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" id="access">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="eyebrow">Mechanism two</p>
      <h2>You never hand us a password.</h2>
      <p class="lead">
        You do not give us a password. Not for Instagram, not for OnlyFans.
      </p>
      <p>
        We work through the official interfaces of the platforms, not through your login details. A
        permission you granted can be withdrawn. A password you handed over cannot.
      </p>
      <ol>
        <li>Instagram publishing runs through Zernio, an authorised interface with server side scheduling.</li>
        <li>OnlyFans chat and vault management runs through Infloww, also an authorised interface.</li>
        <li>
          Our team signs into our own systems with company Google accounts, not with shared logins.
        </li>
        <li>
          Your portal access is its own identity, separate from the staff system. A creator account
          can never technically hold a staff role. Your session is resolved from a signed cookie
          only, never from the request itself, so nobody can look into someone else's data by
          changing an identifier.
        </li>
        <li>
          An administrator can revoke your portal access immediately, effective on the next click
          rather than the next login. Removing you from the portal deletes nothing permanently, it
          sets a date field, so a wrong click is one reset and not a loss.
        </li>
      </ol>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" id="chatting">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="eyebrow">Mechanism three</p>
      <h2>The people writing in your name get audited.</h2>
      <p class="lead">
        You already make the work. What is missing is the selling, and that is the part we do, with
        people who get audited for it.
      </p>
      <p>
        Whoever writes to your fans in your name is reviewed, and not by the person who wrote. Chat
        is the only service that happens in your name, so we built a review track for it that runs
        on a schedule and that nobody on the chat team can switch off.
      </p>
      <ol>
        <li>
          Twice a week an automated run reads the shift plan, pulls the chat log of the last
          finished shift and scores it against a versioned rubric.
        </li>
        <li>
          Only human messages are scored. Every message is classified by source first, automated
          messages serve as context only.
        </li>
        <li>
          There is a fixed pass mark, a median across several scorers, hard fail reasons and a
          measurement of response time.
        </li>
        <li>The chat logs themselves are never stored in the database for this report.</li>
        <li>
          Automatically generated messages additionally pass a hard coded content limit, including
          an age detector that blocks any message hinting at an age under eighteen, and a ban on
          asking for payments outside the platform. The point is to stop an automated message from
          putting your account at risk.
        </li>
      </ol>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>What we do not do.</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>No upfront fee, and no fee before you have earned anything.</li>
        <li>No access to your money. Your payout reaches your account first.</li>
        <li>
          No content published without your release, and no old material kept in rotation once you
          have asked for it to stop.
        </li>
        <li>We do not guarantee your income, and nobody honest can.</li>
      </ul>
      <CtaBlock />
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  .portal-hint {
    max-width: 760px;
    margin: 32px 0;
  }
</style>
